.SH DATE AND TIME CALCULATIONS
If \f[I]DATE\f[R] is not given, defaults to \f[I]now\f[R].
If only one \f[I]DATE\f[R] is set, the first one is assumed to be
\f[I]now\f[R] (or \f[I]1970\f[R] as last fallback).
.PP
To flag \f[I]DATE\f[R] as UNIX time, prepend an \[lq]at\[rq] sign
\[lq]\f[I]\[at]\f[R]\[rq] to it or set \f[B]option \-r\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]GNU date\f[R] accepts most free\-form human\-readable date
strings.
With \f[CR]FreeBSD date\f[R], fewer formats are supported but the input
format may be set with \f[B]option \-f\f[R] \f[I]FORMAT\f[R].
.PP
When no \f[CR]C\-code date\f[R] programme is available, input must be
given as \f[B]ISO\-8601 date\f[R] or \f[B]UNIX time\f[R].
.PP
Standard input is expected to either contain one \f[I]DATE\f[R] string
per line (max two lines), or two ISO\-8601 \f[I]DATES\f[R] separated by
space in a single line.
Input is processed in a best\-effort basis.
.SH DATE AND TIME OUTPUT
Output \[lq]RANGES\[rq] section displays intervals in different units of
time (years, or months, or weeks..).
It also displays a compound time range, considering all units relative
to each other.
.PP
\f[I]Single\-unit\f[R] time periods can be displayed in table format
with \f[B]option \-t\f[R].
The decimal scale factor can be set with command line \f[B]option
\-NUM\f[R] where \f[I]NUM\f[R] is an integer.
The least significant digit of the result is subject to rounding.
.PP
When the last positional parameter \f[I]UNIT\f[R] is exactly one of
\f[I]Y\f[R], \f[I]MO\f[R], \f[I]W\f[R], \f[I]D\f[R], \f[I]H\f[R],
\f[I]M\f[R], or \f[I]S\f[R], only a \f[I]single\-unit\f[R] time interval
is printed to stdout.
.PP
Output \[lq]DATES\[rq] section prints out input as \f[B]UNIX time\f[R]
and \f[B]ISO\-8601 date\f[R] or, if \f[B]option \-R\f[R] is set, as
\f[B]RFC\-5322 date\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Option \-u\f[R] sets environment \[lq]$TZ=UTC\[rq] and operates in
Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) internally.
Note this affects how the \f[CR]C\-code date\f[R] programme processes
dates.
.PP
\f[B]Option \-v\f[R] prints single\-unit ranges alone (e.g.\ \[lq]\f[I]1
day, 24 hours, 1440 mins\f[R]\[rq]).
\f[B]Option \-vv\f[R] prints the compound range alone
(e.g.\ \[lq]\f[I]6Y 11M 01W 06D\f[R]\[rq]), and setting \f[B]\-vvv\f[R]
further removes spaces (e.g.\ \[lq]\f[I]6Y11M01W06D\f[R]\[rq]), AST
style.
.SH SPECIAL DATE FUNCTIONS
\f[B]Option \-e\f[R] prints Easter dates for given \f[I]YEARS\f[R]
(Western Church) and \f[B]option \-ee\f[R] additionally prints Carnaval
and Corpus Christi dates.
.PP
\f[B]Option \-l\f[R] checks if \f[I]YEAR\f[R] is leap.
Set \f[B]option \-v\f[R] to decrease verbosity and exit with a code
signal.
.PP
Note that the ISO\-8601 system assumes proleptic Gregorian calendar,
year zero, and no leap seconds.
.PP
\f[B]Option \-m\f[R] prints lunar phase for \f[I]UTC\f[R] \f[I]DATE\f[R]
(\[lq]\f[I]YYYY[\-MM[\-DD]]\f[R]\[rq]).
Auto expansion takes place on partial \f[I]DATE\f[R] \f[I]input\f[R].
Code snippet adapted from \f[I]NetHack\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Option \-F\f[R] prints the date of the next Friday the 13th.
\f[I]START_DATE\f[R] must be formatted as
\[lq]\f[I]YYYY[\-MM[\-DD]]\f[R]\[rq].
Optionally, set \f[I]day\-in\-week\f[R] and \f[I]day\-in\-month\f[R]
(e.g.\ \[lq]\f[I]Sun\f[R] \f[I]01\f[R] [\f[I]START_DATE\f[R]]\[rq]).
.PP
Set \f[B]options \-FF\f[R] to print the following ten matches.
.SH TIMEZONE OFFSETS
Environment $TZ is read as \f[B]POSIX offset\f[R] when it holds a
positive or negative decimal number, such as \[lq]\f[I]+03\f[R]\[rq] (or
even \[lq]\f[I]UTC+03\f[R]\[rq]).
.PP
\f[B]POSIX\f[R] timezone definition by the $TZ variable takes a
different form from \f[B]ISO\-8601\f[R] standards, so that
\[lq]\f[I]ISO\f[R] \f[I]UTC\-03\f[R]\[rq] is equivalent to setting
\[lq]\f[I]$TZ=UTC+03\f[R]\[rq].
.PP
Importantly, $TZ \f[B]POSIX offsets\f[R] are often the inverse of
\f[B]ISO\-8601\f[R] \f[I]UTC\f[R] \f[I]values\f[R] seen in timestamps,
so that:
.IP \[bu] 2
\[lq]$TZ=+03\[rq] corresponds to an offset of \[lq]ISO UTC\-03\[rq]
(West of UTC)
.IP \[bu] 2
\[lq]$TZ=\-03\[rq] corresponds to an offset of \[lq]ISO UTC+03\[rq]
(East of UTC)
.PP
Timezone names and IDs (e.g.\ \[lq]\f[I]America/Sao_Paulo\f[R]\[rq]) may
be parsed by \f[CR]C\-code date\f[R] when it is leveraged to process
user input.
.SH REFINEMENT RULES
\f[B]Compound time range\f[R] calculations may utilise some rules to
minimise excessive granularity in the resulting units.
.PP
This happens mainly when dealing with end\-of\-month and
start\-of\-month date combinations and different month lengths, or
unusual offsets.
.PP
The script\[cq]s logic strives to follow \f[I]Hroptatyr\[cq]s\f[R]
\f[CR]C\-code datediff\f[R] refinement rules on compound range.
.PP
Remaining observable discrepancies are mostly due to rolling up months,
and the rate of occurrence varies depending on input dates at month
boundaries.
.PP
Shell arithmetics are correctly verified against implementations of
\f[CR]c\-code\f[R] \f[CR]date\f[R] and \f[CR]datediff\f[R].
.PP
Check source code and project repository for more information and
details.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]DATE_CMD\f[R]
Path for the \f[CR]C\-code date\f[R] binary.
.RS
.PP
\f[CR]GNU\f[R], \f[CR]BSD\f[R], \f[CR]AST\f[R], and \f[CR]Busybox\f[R]
\f[CR]date\f[R] are supported.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]TZ\f[R]
\f[CR]POSIX\f[R] timezone offset.
Numeric offset must be in the format \[lq]\f[I][+|\-]HH[:MM]\f[R]\[rq],
or sometimes \[lq]\f[I]UTC[+|\-]HH[:MM]\f[R]\[rq].
